WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.369
Text validation in C starts with a
practical reminder: bytes, characters,

2
00:00:04.449 --> 00:00:06.966
and displayed text are not always the same
thing.

3
00:00:07.046 --> 00:00:10.301
Many APIs operate on byte buffers or
null-terminated strings.

4
00:00:10.381 --> 00:00:14.618
The program has to decide what encoding it
accepts and whether its length rules are

5
00:00:14.698 --> 00:00:17.706
based on bytes, characters, fields, or
display behavior.

6
00:00:17.786 --> 00:00:21.839
Embedded null bytes can create different
interpretations of the same buffer.

7
00:00:21.919 --> 00:00:24.928
A length-delimited parser may see bytes
after a zero byte,

8
00:00:25.008 --> 00:00:27.648
while C string functions may stop at the
first null.

9
00:00:27.728 --> 00:00:32.027
Validation should define whether embedded
null bytes are allowed and ensure later

10
00:00:32.107 --> 00:00:33.949
code uses the same interpretation.

11
00:00:34.029 --> 00:00:36.976
Control characters and whitespace need
explicit rules.

12
00:00:37.056 --> 00:00:40.434
A newline, tab, carriage return, leading
space, trailing space,

13
00:00:40.514 --> 00:00:44.628
or nonprinting byte may be acceptable in
one field and unacceptable in another.

14
00:00:44.708 --> 00:00:46.980
Trimming or normalizing text can be
useful,

15
00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:50.805
but only when the rule matches the data
format and the program's meaning.

16
00:00:50.885 --> 00:00:53.772
Case normalization should be designed for
the use case.

17
00:00:53.852 --> 00:00:57.659
A case-insensitive option name, a
user-visible label, a protocol token,

18
00:00:57.739 --> 00:01:01.484
and a locale-sensitive text field may all
have different expectations.

19
00:01:01.564 --> 00:01:04.819
Code should not casually change case if
that changes identity,

20
00:01:04.899 --> 00:01:06.987
comparison behavior, or audit meaning.

21
00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:10.321
Character allowlists work best when they
are tied to the field.

22
00:01:10.401 --> 00:01:14.331
A simple identifier, a decimal number, a
path segment, a configuration key,

23
00:01:14.411 --> 00:01:17.850
and a free-text note need different
allowed sets and length rules.

24
00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:22.167
Documenting the encoding and character
rule keeps validation consistent across

25
00:01:22.247 --> 00:01:24.580
parsing, storage, comparison, and logging.
